 "_": "Lifted from gamedata/Runtime 2.exe by tools/exetables.py - do not hand-edit; run the tool. These tables are compiled into the executable and are NOT in the shipped gamedata/ data, which is why a replica engine needs this file.",
 "table": "vm_announce",
 "documented_in": "docs/SCRIPT_VM.md",
 "about": "Which `IAM\\*.TAG` domain each VM handler announces its operand to.\n\nRead from the assembly by `tools/vm_announce.py`: every announcing handler\npasses a section name to `Dbg_LogTagged` (sub_40EC70), and that call sits\nbefore the debug window's `if (hWnd)` - which is what makes the golden\ntraces possible at all, since the engine narrates itself through\n`GetPrivateProfileStringA` whether or not anything is listening.\n\n`field` is WHICH operand is announced, and it is not always the first:\n`scene.load(area, scene)` announces the **scene**, `inventory.add(list,\nobject)` the **object**. 49 handlers announce; every other opcode is\nsilent, and a reader must treat that as silence rather than as a default.\n\nEmitted as data because the alternative is a hand-written map, and a\nhand-written one was wrong three ways within an hour of being written:\n`scx.play` (57) and `music.play` (103) announce **nothing**, and\n`scx.play.actor.wait` (60) announces CHARACTERS - the actor - not the\nobject. Deriving it removes the guess.",
 "checks": [
  {
   "what": "announcing handlers",
   "got": 49,
   "want": 49
  },
  {
   "what": "...where the assembly and the field map agree",
   "got": 49,
   "want": 49
  },
  {
   "what": "opcodes that announce nothing",
   "got": 104,
   "want": 104
  },
  {
   "what": "scx.play (57) is silent",
   "got": true,
   "want": true
  },
  {
   "what": "music.play (103) is silent",
   "got": true,
   "want": true
  },
  {
   "what": "scx.play.actor.wait (60) announces",
   "got": "CHARACTERS",
   "want": "CHARACTERS"
  },
  {
   "what": "scene.load (71) announces its SECOND field",
   "got": 1,
   "want": 1
  }
 ],
